==================================================
Lab 7: Blind SQL Injection with Conditional Responses
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

All the earlier labs had one thing in common: the result of the SQL query showed up directly on the page, either
as product data or as an error message. This lab is different. The query result is never shown anywhere. There
is no UNION trick that displays extra rows, and no error message that leaks anything.

Instead, the only thing that changes on the page is a small, indirect clue: whether or not the text “Welcome
back” appears. This kind of attack is called blind SQL injection, because we cannot see the data directly, we can
only ask the database true or false questions, one at a time, and watch for a small visible difference in the
response to tell which answer we got.

Goal: without ever seeing the password directly, figure out the administrator’s password character by character,
purely by asking yes or no questions.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

This time the vulnerable input is not a URL parameter like category, it is a cookie called TrackingId. Every
visitor gets a tracking cookie automatically, and the website uses this cookie’s value inside a SQL query behind
the scenes, most likely to check if this visitor is a returning, recognized user. If the value matches something
in the database, the website shows “Welcome back” somewhere on the page.

Since this cookie value is used inside a query without being checked or cleaned, we can inject SQL into it, the
same basic idea as every earlier lab, just through a different entry point.

=============================
WHAT IS A BLIND CONDITION

A blind condition is a piece of SQL we attach to the existing query, written so that it only adds something true
or false, without changing what data comes back. If the condition we add is true, the page behaves exactly as
it would for a normal visitor, and “Welcome back” appears. If the condition is false, that message disappears.

Nothing about the actual data is shown. We only learn whether our guess was right or wrong, which is just a
single bit of information per request, yes or no.

============================================
STEP 1: CONFIRM THE INJECTION POINT WORKS

Before asking anything useful, we first prove that we can control the query’s truth value at all.

We send a condition we already know is true, something like: does a table called users exist, and does it have
at least one row. If “Welcome back” appears, our injection is working and the database is treating our input as
real SQL.

We then also check if a user named administrator actually exists in that table, the same way, by asking a true
or false question and watching for “Welcome back”.

============================================
STEP 2: FIND THE PASSWORD LENGTH

Before we can find each character, we need to know how long the password actually is, otherwise we would not
know when to stop checking positions.

To do this, we repeatedly ask: is the length of the administrator’s password greater than 1, then greater than
2, then greater than 3, and so on. Each time the answer is true (“Welcome back” shows up), we move to the next
number. The exact moment the answer flips to false, that number tells us the real password length. For example,
if “length greater than 20” is true but “length greater than 21” is false, the password is exactly 21 characters
long. In this lab specifically, the real password length turns out to be a fixed number, but we discover it the
same way regardless.

============================================
STEP 3: FIND EACH CHARACTER, ONE POSITION AT A TIME

Once we know the length, we need to figure out the password’s actual characters. Since the password itself is
invisible to us, we can only guess one character at a time, at one specific position, and ask: is the character
at this position equal to this specific letter or digit.

To do this, we use a function called SUBSTRING, which pulls out one character from a specific position in the
password. We combine this with an equals check against a guessed character. If the guess is correct, “Welcome
back” appears, confirming that exact character at that exact position. If wrong, it disappears, and we try the
next possible character.

We repeat this for every position in the password, trying every lowercase letter and every digit until we find
the one that makes the condition true, before moving on to the next position.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and intercept the request carrying the TrackingId cookie using Burp Suite.
Modify the cookie value to add a true condition, and confirm “Welcome back” still appears.
Modify it to add a false condition, and confirm “Welcome back” disappears.
Confirm the users table and the administrator user exist, using true or false conditions the same way.
Test increasing password lengths until the condition becomes false, revealing the exact password length.
Use Burp Intruder to automate testing every character at every position, using SUBSTRING and checking for
“Welcome back” in each response.
Once every position’s character is found, combine them into the full password, and log in manually as
administrator using that password.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests, and the string library, which gives easy access
to a ready-made list of lowercase letters and digits, saving us from typing them out manually.

It asks for the base address of the lab, and removes any trailing slash so addresses can be joined cleanly later.

A session is created, and a single request is sent to the home page just to receive a genuine TrackingId cookie
from the server, exactly as a normal visitor would get one. This original cookie value is saved, since every
injected payload needs to be built on top of this real value to keep the rest of the query working normally.

If no TrackingId cookie comes back at all, the script stops immediately, since there is nothing to inject into.

A helper function called send_condition is defined. It takes any SQL condition as plain text, builds a cookie
value using the original tracking ID, a single quote, AND, the condition wrapped in parentheses, and compares it
against the letter a. It then sends this as the cookie and returns True or False depending on whether “Welcome
back” shows up in the response. This function is reused for every true or false question asked throughout the
rest of the script, instead of rewriting the same request logic repeatedly.

The script first uses this helper to confirm that a basic true condition about the users table returns true,
proving the injection point works.

It then confirms that a user named administrator actually exists, using the same helper function.

Next, it searches for the password length. It loops through numbers starting from 1, each time asking if the
password length is greater than that number. As soon as one of these checks comes back false, that number is
saved as the actual password length, and the loop stops immediately.

After the length is known, the script begins the character-guessing phase. It prepares a list of all lowercase
letters and all digits to try as possible characters.

For every position in the password, from the first character to the last, it tries every character in that
list one at a time. For each attempt, it builds a condition using SUBSTRING to extract just the character at the
current position, and checks if it equals the character currently being tested. If “Welcome back” appears, that
character is recorded as correct for that position, and the script immediately stops trying further characters
for that position, moving on to the next one.

If no character out of the entire list matches at some position, the script stops early and reports that nothing
was found, since continuing further would not make sense without knowing that position’s character.

Once every position has a confirmed character, they are all joined together into the final complete password,
which is printed out at the end, along with a reminder to log in manually using the administrator username and
this extracted password.

=============================
WHY WE USE A SEPARATE HELPER FUNCTION

Since almost every step in this lab repeats the exact same action, sending a request with a modified cookie and
checking if “Welcome back” appears, writing this as one reusable function avoids repeating the same lines of
code over and over. Every true or false question in the entire script, regardless of what it is actually asking,
flows through this same single function.

=============================
WHY THIS IS SLOWER THAN EARLIER LABS

In earlier labs, a single well-crafted request could reveal actual data directly. Here, every single character
of the password requires potentially testing up to thirty six different possibilities, one request at a time,
before moving to the next character. For a password around twenty characters long, this adds up to several
hundred individual requests just for the character-guessing phase alone, which is why blind injection is
considerably slower than techniques where data is directly visible.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is still the same root cause as every previous lab, user-controlled input, this time coming
from a cookie instead of a URL parameter, is joined directly into a SQL query without being checked. The
difference here is only in how the result of that injected query becomes visible to us, through a subtle
behavioral difference on the page instead of through directly displayed data.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that cookie values, exactly like any other user input, can never change the
structure or logic of a SQL query.

Other good practices:

Avoid exposing any observable difference in application behavior that depends on the result of a database
query involving unsanitized input, since even a small difference like a changed greeting message can be enough
for an attacker to extract data bit by bit.
Apply strict validation on all input sources, not just URL parameters, since cookies, headers, and other hidden
fields are just as capable of reaching a vulnerable query.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

Blind SQL injection does not show any data directly, it only reveals whether a condition is true or false through
some small behavioral difference in the response.
Extracting data this way requires first confirming the injection works, then finding the length of the target
value, then testing every character at every position individually.
This technique works on any input that reaches a SQL query, not just URL parameters, cookies and other hidden
values are just as vulnerable if they are not properly sanitized.
Blind injection is slower and requires far more requests than attacks where data is shown directly, but it is
just as capable of fully extracting sensitive information given enough time.
